    "narrative": "The lineup for today\u2019s matchup features a heavy reliance on speed and favorable pitching matchups to bolster our Roto standing. Our most potent options are **Brice Turang** (73.39) and **Corbin Carroll** (74.49), both of whom benefit from exceptional situational factors. **Corbin Carroll** in particular is positioned perfectly in the #3 spot; his matchup against Woodruff is negated by his proficiency against right-handed pitching and a 100% stolen base opportunity score against a slow delivery, making him our primary engine for the day. Similarly, **Brice Turang**\u2019s 73.39 score is driven by a juicy matchup against Kelly\u2019s 5.47 xERA and a high-leverage leadoff spot. **Josh Naylor** (71.12) rounds out our elite tier, taking full advantage of a weak outing by Bieber to anchor our middle-order production.\n\nBeyond our top-tier starters, **Kyle Karros** (63.92) stands out as an intriguing play, jumping into the utility spot with a significant boost from a park factor of +14%. While **James Wood** (52.66) and **Austin Riley** (52.57) provide respectable utility, we are wary of their matchups against tough arms like Ashcraft and Manaea, respectively. The decision to bench **Travis Bazzana** (48.88) was the closest call of the morning; despite his attractive leadoff placement, his overall efficiency projection fell just short of our starters. We are continuing to monitor the status of **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Tyler Soderstrom**, both of whom remain sidelined on the IL, limiting our depth in the corner infield slots.\n\nAs we head into the games, we are cognizant of the risks posed by elite catchers, specifically impacting the stolen base projections for **James Wood** and **Kyle Karros**. We have opted to bench **Isaac Paredes** (48.51) to preserve our game caps, as his upside against Rasmussen is capped by an unfavorable park factor. We will be closely tracking **Dalton Rushing** and **Alex Freeland**, who face a tougher test against Peralta; their lower scores reflect the reality of a difficult platoon split and the inherent volatility of the bottom-of-the-order hitting positions. We are fully committed to this configuration, prioritizing the high-speed profiles of our outfielders to keep our SB category competitive.",
